        "a": "Commonly constructed using exchange-traded funds (ETFs)",
        "b": "Individual securities, mutual funds, exchange-traded funds, or custom assets"
      }
    ]
  },
  "verdict": "A robo-advisor fits a reader who wants low-cost automated investing for a defined goal and accepts little human contact; a human adviser fits a reader who needs advice beyond investing or wants a person to call. If the human is a registered investment adviser, the duty of care and loyalty is the same as a robo-advisor's; if the person is a broker or dual registrant, a different standard may apply, shown in Form CRS. This outcome changes if an automated platform provides a hybrid human adviser at your balance level, or if the human is broker-only. Read both firms' relationship summaries and Form ADV Part 2 disclosures to compare all-in costs and human access before opening an account.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Robo-Advisor or Financial Advisor: Which Fits Your Situation",
      "content": "Pick a robo-advisor if you want low-cost automated investing for a defined goal and accept little human contact; pick a human adviser if you need advice beyond investing or want a person to talk to. Your choice between a robo-advisor and a traditional human advisor depends on the complexity of your financial profile and your preferences for personal support. If your goal is straightforward wealth accumulation for retirement using a diversified portfolio, an automated platform provides structured management without the administrative overhead of individual office appointments. If you require comprehensive tax planning, estate document coordination, or guidance through complicated business sales, a qualified human advisor can supply personalized oversight that algorithms cannot deliver.\n\nAccording to the [SEC Investor Bulletin on Robo-Advisers](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-45) published by the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) Office of Investor Education and Advocacy (OIEA), a robo-advisor is an automated digital investment advisory program. These platforms collect your financial goals, investment time horizon, household income, other assets, and risk tolerance through an online questionnaire. The software then uses that collected information to build and manage an investment portfolio for you. Because the onboarding workflow is automated, robo-advisors generally present lower fees and smaller account minimums than traditional advisory programs.\n\nHuman investment advisers operate differently during the initial evaluation process. As detailed by [Investor.gov on Investment Advisers](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/working-investment-professional/investment-advisers), an investment adviser is a firm or person that provides investment advice about securities for compensation. These professionals typically review your entire financial balance sheet, discuss personal priorities, and help identify unstated risks or opportunities. While an automated program relies entirely on the answers entered into an intake form, a human professional can ask clarifying questions, adjust assumptions during market turbulence, and coordinate multi-faceted financial strategies. Understanding [what a financial advisor is worth](/guides/financial-advisor-worth-it/) starts with matching these specific service capabilities to your financial needs."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Side-by-Side Comparison of Advisory Models",
      "content": "Evaluating automated programs and human advisors side by side clarifies their operational differences. Both categories manage investment securities for individual clients, yet their workflows, fee mechanisms, and client obligations differ. The following table contrasts a robo-advisor with a human investment adviser who is a registered investment adviser.\n\n| Feature | Robo-Advisor | Human Registered Investment Adviser |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Primary focus | Automated digital portfolio management | Ongoing portfolio oversight and comprehensive planning |\n| Intake procedure | Structured online questionnaire | Direct personal consultation and financial discovery |\n| Portfolio building blocks | Frequently exchange-traded funds (ETFs) | Individual equities, bonds, mutual funds, or ETFs |\n| Human communication | Technical help only, tiered balance access, or hybrid | Regular scheduled meetings, phone calls, and email |\n| Legal standard | Fiduciary duty of care and loyalty | Fiduciary duty of care and loyalty |\n| Disciplinary and background checks | Form ADV Part 1 and Form CRS | Form ADV Part 1, Part 2B supplements, and Form CRS |\n| Client responsibility | Must manually update profile details online | Updates gathered during periodic client reviews |\n\nSelecting between these formats requires balancing automated simplicity against customized human guidance. Investors managing single-account goals often find automated tools sufficient, while those managing complex trusts or taxable accounts benefit from human consultation. You can track your total accumulated wealth as you evaluate these structures by using our [net worth calculator](/net-worth/)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What Robo-Advisors and Financial Advisors Cost You",
      "content": "Advisory fees represent an ongoing expense that reduces your net investment returns over time. Comparing costs between automated platforms and personal advisers requires examining both fee structures and auxiliary account expenses. Most traditional investment advisers charge an asset-based fee calculated as a percentage of the total assets held in your account. As noted in the [Investor.gov Relationship Summary (Form CRS)](https://www.investor.gov/CRS) guidance, an asset-based fee compensates the professional for ongoing portfolio monitoring, but it means the dollar amount you pay increases as your portfolio grows.\n\nRobo-advisors utilize diverse pricing frameworks, ranging from asset-based percentage fees to recurring subscriptions. The [SEC Investor Bulletin on Subscription-Based Advisory Fees](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/subscription-based-advisory-fees) notes that subscription pricing entails a set recurring fee, typically billed monthly. For smaller robo-adviser accounts, that fee might be $3, $5, or $10 per month for investment management. Many automated firms use these fixed monthly fees to market advisory services to newer investors with smaller account balances. However, on very small balances, a monthly fee of $5 or $10 can represent a high percentage of your overall assets, which makes calculating the relative impact necessary.\n\nAdvisory management charges do not encompass all investment expenses. Both human advisers and robo-advisers invest client money into underlying funds that carry internal operational expenses. The SEC bulletin on robo-advisers notes that many digital programs build portfolios using exchange-traded funds (ETFs), which deduct ongoing management expenses from fund performance. Similarly, custodial platforms may assess transaction costs, wire fees, or account administration charges. For detailed comparisons of conventional percentage models and fixed advisory retainers, read our analysis of [flat-fee vs AUM advisors](/compare/flat-fee-vs-aum-based-financial-advisors/), or explore specific automated choices in our guide to the [best robo-advisors](/roundup/best-robo-advisors/)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "How Much Human Contact You Get with Each Option",
      "content": "The level of personal contact varies widely across advisory options and should dictate which path you select. When working with a traditional human financial advisor, direct personal dialogue serves as the foundation of the relationship. You talk directly with a professional to discuss cash flow changes, family milestones, retirement timelines, and emotional anxieties during market volatility. This access allows the adviser to gather qualitative context that standard web forms cannot capture.\n\nRobo-advisory platforms deliver varying levels of human communication. The SEC bulletin on robo-advisers explains that some digital programs offer access to an investment professional, often described as a hybrid or bionic model. Other automated platforms restrict human contact entirely to technical support staff who can fix login glitches or explain dashboard features but cannot provide personalized financial advice. Furthermore, some automated firms condition access to human professionals on maintaining a minimum account size, leaving smaller account holders with purely digital interfaces.\n\nUnlike traditional advisory relationships, digital programs typically lack an initial conversation with a person to assess your personal needs. Instead, the algorithm generates recommendations strictly from the factual prompts contained in its online questionnaire. The SEC bulletin points out that robo-adviser recommendations are limited by the information requested, and the burden to update your profile data falls squarely on you. If your marital status, tax bracket, or career plans change, the algorithm cannot incorporate those developments until you log in and manually alter your account settings."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Duty of Care Depends on How Your Advisor Is Registered",
      "content": "The legal standard of conduct governing your investment professional depends entirely on their regulatory registration. Many investors assume every financial professional operates under the same legal obligations, but regulatory classifications establish distinct legal duties. In a public statement on [Regulation Best Interest and the Investment Adviser Fiduciary Duty](https://www.sec.gov/newsroom/speeches-statements/clayton-regulation-best-interest-investment-adviser-fiduciary-duty), former SEC Chairman Jay Clayton explained that the investment adviser fiduciary duty is comprised of a duty of care and a duty of loyalty. This fiduciary framework applies to registered investment advisers, requiring them to act in the best interest of their clients and avoid placing their own financial interests ahead of the client's interests.\n\nRobo-advisors registered with the SEC operate as registered investment advisers, which means they owe clients an adviser fiduciary duty of care and loyalty. Similarly, human professionals who operate as registered investment advisers are held to this same fiduciary standard. However, many individual financial professionals do not operate solely as investment advisers. Some professionals are registered representatives of broker-dealers, while others are dual registrants licensed as both brokers and investment advisers. For broker-dealers, Regulation Best Interest establishes the governing standard of conduct, requiring brokers to act in the best interest of retail customers when making investment recommendations.\n\nDetermining which standard applies to your advisory relationship requires reviewing regulatory disclosure documents. According to the [SEC Investor Bulletin on Form ADV](https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-71), retail investors should review Form ADV Part 2 and the Form CRS relationship summary. Form ADV Part 2A serves as an adviser's primary narrative brochure, detailing advisory business practices, fee schedules, conflict points, and disciplinary history across 18 standardized items. Form CRS explicitly states whether a firm is an investment adviser, a broker-dealer, or a dual registrant. You can verify firm registrations and review public filings on the SEC [Investment Adviser Public Disclosure (IAPD)](https://adviserinfo.sec.gov) database or check individual broker backgrounds through the FINRA [BrokerCheck](https://brokercheck.finra.org) portal. Understanding [what a fiduciary is](/guides/what-is-a-fiduciary/) provides essential context when evaluating these legal relationships."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Who Each Option Is Not For and What Would Change the Answer",
      "content": "A robo-advisor is not suitable for individuals who need customized estate planning, complex business succession guidance, or direct phone access to an adviser during market downturns. If your financial situation requires coordinating trust distributions, minimizing corporate tax liabilities, or managing concentrated stock positions, an automated algorithm cannot handle those nuanced demands. Conversely, an ongoing human registered investment adviser is not suitable for an investor who holds a small account balance, pursues a single straightforward retirement goal, and wants basic index investing without paying high fees.\n\nSpecific operational changes can flip this general advisory recommendation. If an automated robo-advisory platform offers a hybrid service tier that grants direct access to a dedicated human adviser at your specific balance level, the robo-advisor may satisfy your need for personal communication at a competitive price. In that scenario, paying higher traditional advisory costs becomes harder to justify. If you want to understand automated services in depth, read our overview of [what a robo-advisor is](/guides/what-is-a-robo-advisor/).\n\nConversely, if a human professional you interview is registered exclusively as a broker-dealer representative rather than an investment adviser, the regulatory baseline shifts. Because a broker is held to Regulation Best Interest rather than the comprehensive fiduciary duty of care and loyalty under the Investment Advisers Act, an investor seeking an ongoing fiduciary partnership might prefer a registered robo-adviser. Checking Form CRS confirms whether your prospective advisor operates as an investment adviser or a broker. Reviewing your personal planning targets with our [retirement calculator](/retirement/) will help clarify which advisory model supports your goals."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Our Takeaway on Picking the Right Advisory Structure",
      "content": "A robo-advisor fits a reader who wants low-cost automated investing for a defined goal and accepts little human contact, while a human adviser fits a reader who needs advice beyond investing or wants a person to call. When the human professional is a registered investment adviser, the duty of care and loyalty matches what an automated robo-adviser owes under federal law. If that professional operates as a broker or dual registrant, a different legal standard may govern transactions, as outlined in Form CRS. This assessment changes if an automated platform provides hybrid human consultation at your account balance, or if a prospective human adviser operates under a broker-only registration.\n\nTake action by examining public regulatory disclosures before opening an account with any firm. Search the firm and individual professional on the SEC IAPD website to review their Form ADV Part 2 narrative brochure and Form CRS relationship summary. Compare total all-in costs, including advisory fees, fund expenses, and platform charges, and verify the exact scope of human communication provided in your written advisory contract."
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "Are robo-advisors better than financial advisors?",
      "answer": "Robo-advisors are better for investors who want automated, low-cost investment management for a defined goal and do not require personal financial planning. Human financial advisors are better for individuals with complex assets, tax planning requirements, or estate planning needs who want ongoing personal communication. The right option depends on your account size and the breadth of services you require."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is a robo-advisor a good idea?",
      "answer": "A robo-advisor is a good idea if you are seeking a convenient, lower-cost way to build a diversified portfolio using exchange-traded funds without managing daily trades yourself. However, it may not be a good idea if you need comprehensive tax guidance, customized financial planning, or reassurance from an experienced person during volatile market conditions."
    },
    {
      "question": "Robo-advisor vs financial planner: what is the difference?",
      "answer": "The regulatory sources reviewed do not provide a formal legal definition for the generic title financial planner. A robo-advisor is an automated digital investment advisory program that builds and manages securities portfolios based on questionnaire responses. Many human financial planners operate as registered investment advisers who deliver broad financial planning alongside portfolio monitoring. Read each professional's Form CRS and Form ADV Part 2 brochure to examine their specific credentials and service scopes."
    }
  ],
